ned that the loot system  works  more akin to Diablo in that enemies drop  randomized loot with different rarities  and stat benefits and in my first  playthrough right I simply could not  beat the boss so I went back through the  level once and I killed all the enemies  and I picked up another round of loot I  got a bunch of new armor pieces and some  rings and I managed to beat the boss  after I did that and that felt good  these enemies that I killed they still  had value even after I'd moved on from  them and by contrast in a Souls game  there's never really a reason to go back  and kill enemies that you've already  progressed past in ATAR randomized loot  makes even boring enemies exciting  because you never know when they're  going to drop something neat and you  know even though the enemies are really  easy to run past it's not a good idea to  do that because your character is going  to be underpowered  as a result what's more because the game  actually motivated me to go back and  kill some enemies  I found a side path that I missed before  down this side path there were a bunch  of new enemies and I ended up finding a  great sword awesome the great sword  requires two hands and it's slow but it  makes up for it because it crushes the  enemy guards and it has this fantastic  charge attack where you dash forward and  do an uppercut and that's another thing  I need to mention every weapon you find  in this demo is really important the  great sword for example it's unmatched  when you need to take out a group of  enemies fast the bow is excellent when  you want to lure enemies one by one and  fight them carefully or if you want to  pick them off at a distance the sword  and shield are fantastic for one-on-one  fights especially when you're doing  fights that you're unfamiliar with  triangle with some weapons allows you to  use a sort of weapon art for example the  shield has a frost parry  if you find the axe you can dual wield a  short sword and axe for more dps and  less defense triangle with this weapon  combination buffs your weapons for a  short time like using pine resin in a  souls game and the cool thing about  weapon arts like frost shield and  electric dual wield is that they  recharge when you play a certain way for  example you get more charges of frost  shield when you block attacks and you  get more charges of electric weapons  when you damage things so when you have  those weapons equipped it rewards you  for playing to your weapons strengths  these are just all these really cool  design choices that they've made that  really make you admire their attention  to detail so I found myself using every  weapon combination to deal with  different situations making my character  feel like she could adapt to anything if  I just gave it some thought and there  were a lot of situations where you had  to think here for example the best way  to take out these warriors was luring  them to the traps this group of enemies  rushes at you after you clear a wave of  arrows and there's no retreat here so I  died a couple of times because I  couldn't reposition myself the great so  what I found was one of the best weapons  to use here because you weren't forced  backwards when you were using it you  just sort of slaughtered the enemies in  your path and you took a bit of damage  but they didn't force you back into the  arrows so that was good  this next combination of enemies was  really tough because the big brute like  enemy he hid behind the smaller shield  enemies and it was difficult to get in  close because the threat of the big  enemy was always looming over you while  you tactically tried to get rid of the  little guy as shields and yeah this was  tough this next combination of enemies  was arranged wizard and three assassin  class skeletons best way to take them  out just overpower the skeletons before  they can hit you and when you make it  past all that assuming this trap doesn't  kill you if you're listening closely you  start to hear the sound of guitar  a crackling fire I actually love that  the bonfire in this game has a really  really audible sound cue it means that  you're less likely to miss the bonfire  in souls games do you ever remember like  being on your last Estus flask and  praying for a bonfire and then it might  be in a room but you don't know it's in  that room so you go into a random room  you die and then you realize that the  bonfire was right next to you that whole  time in a tur well you can hear the  bonfire before you see it so you know  which direction you should go and that's  a cool little touch next to another  bonfire is the only NPC in this demo who  gives us a fancy blue sword and a bit of  exposition about the world and its story  ater  the namesake of the game is a poison  that corrupts everything it touches the  devs say that eita is what poisoned ye  gracel which is the World Tree and  source of all life by extension since  you gotta sell is corrupted so to his  man and we play as the shieldmaiden  somehow unaffected by this blight that  effects man and we're on a quest to save  the world tree from corruption because  if the world tree dies then everything  dies but I feel like if you thought the  story was that simple that you'd be  wrong from what I've seen every item has  a description in the inventory screen  that flashes out the game's story a  little bit more just like souls moving  on after hearing some basic story our  character receives a point of favor  which appears in the top left it comes  with the message the gods do not favor  those who fall which implies truthfully  that you lose this favor if you die now  the favor system is really cool because  while you have a point of favor in your  top left your character appears to do  more damage  and take less damage however if you  don't want to run the risk of losing  this favor and this buff then you can  invest this in stat points instead which  remain after death  so it's this call toss-up between short  term power or long term potential I can  just see it now like someone is going to  get every favor point in the game  they're never going to die and they're  going to just become this super strong  powerhouse who can't dial they lose  everything I can just see that happening  speaking of dying though let's talk  bosses which arguably was the best part  of the demo for me the again Walker let  me tell you they're not pulling any of  their punches with this guy frankly I'm  kind of amazed that a boss this hard is  in the PSX demo of the game one of the  devs mentioned I was one of the first  people to beat him and that's probably  because I sat down with the game for  over two hours you know to give you guys  an idea of how long it took me I played  for about two hours and the boss  definitely took me more than one hour so  at least 50% of my time was spent  fighting this boss the boss fight starts  off pretty slow it just lets you get  familiar with his melee attacks he has a  short stab and the slash and while I  didn't try to parry him I've heard  that's a really good technique at 90%  health he powers up and he develops a  bunch of magic attacks he has this  triple magic circle that you should  start running early for if you notice  him casting it or you can dodge through  it if you notice it too late he has a  huge powerup AoE attack that does almost  all of your health if you get caught in  it but it lets you hit him a lot of  times afterwards and lastly he develops  this bloody skull attack that is his  most annoying move it's tough to dodge  it does a lot of damage and in Phase  three it gets even worse phase three  starts at 50% health he summons for  apparitions to fight you and they're a  little bit weaker than the other  it's not they're damaged that's the  problem though these enemies will now  attempt to stun lock you while you fight  the boss because remember that skull  attack I mentioned well now the skull  summons these apparitions as well so  let's just take stock of what's going on  here you have this boss with a huge  health pool a variety of ranged and  close-range attacks and at 50% health he  summons enemies every single time he  does the skull attack this guy is no  joke my one gripe with it actually is  how tough it can get when he repeatedly  spams the skull attack because if you're  lucky he'll never use the skull attack  and if you're unlucky he'll just use it  over and over again so here's what I did  for this boss I would equip the great  sword and I would use it to take out the  groups of enemies as soon as I could and  then I would pull out my dual wielding  axe and sword and I would just go ham at  him and try to burst him down when there  were no enemies around and eventually it  worked if you guys want to watch me play  through the whole demo I actually  recorded a live playthrough a let's play  initially I was only going to make this  available to patrons but this game isn't  out yet there's not much footage online  and you all deserve to see how cool it  is so if you want to watch me struggle  with the game and struggle to do a let's  play because I'm not really used to it  then click the link in the description  and maybe you'll even think about  becoming a patron because I'm excited to  announce that patrons in the $5 tier  should be getting a playthrough of games  that I'm playing maybe once a week for  supporting the channel which is cool so  I'm hyped for this game clearly don't  have much bad to say about it and I  don't want to be too critical on the  game yet because it's not out so we'll  wait for it to come out then we'll do a  review and we'll talk about how good or  bad it is and yeah so subscribe look  forward to that and bear in mind that  the devs have to flesh out so much to do  with the game they're probably still  developing the story they're going to  add in more weapons and armor sets a way  to level up a way to customize your  characters playstyle and all the little  tweaks to N  and animations and environments so yeah  sometime in 2016  Eric am I going to have a good time so  thanks for watching guys and I'll see  you in the next one Cheers  you
 

